 To find the probability that at least one woman is selected when three people are chosen from a group of 10 (7 men and 3 women), we can calculate the probability of the opposite event (selecting no women, i.e., all men) and subtract it from 1. The total number of ways to select 3 people from 10 is given by the combination formula \(C(n, k) = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\), where \(n\) is the total number of items, and \(k\) is the number of items to choose. The number of ways to select 3 men out of 7 is \(C(7, 3)\). The total number of ways to select any 3 people out of 10 is \(C(10, 3)\). The probability of selecting no women (all men) is \(\frac{C(7, 3)}{C(10, 3)}\). Therefore, the probability of selecting at least one woman is \(1 - \frac{C(7, 3)}{C(10, 3)}\). </think> <think> \[
